00:20:05,559 --> 00:20:16,919
And so now, actually, you see the rally, where the IPO shows that many institutional investors

229
00:20:16,920 --> 00:20:25,240
who are very rational, who are very predictable in what can be delivered with their investment

230
00:20:25,240 --> 00:20:27,360
in the future are very clear.

231
00:20:27,360 --> 00:20:33,519
So they definitely bet on the big commercialization.

232
00:20:33,519 --> 00:20:35,360
That's a matter only at time.

233
00:20:35,360 --> 00:20:36,860
It could be Unitary.

234
00:20:36,640 --> 00:20:38,920
It could be another type of company.

235
00:20:38,920 --> 00:20:47,160
But the general ecosystem is there to continually support such sort of commercialization

236
00:20:47,160 --> 00:20:52,720
with more industrial use and also household use.

237
00:20:52,720 --> 00:20:57,640
Just now you mentioned the founder of Unitary, Mr. Wang Xingxing.

238
00:20:57,640 --> 00:21:03,120
Here is what he said really a day after the IPO at the World Robot Conference.

239
00:21:03,119 --> 00:21:11,799
He said that the so-called chat GPT moment will be there when a robot is placed in an unfamiliar home

240
00:21:11,799 --> 00:21:17,199
and can complete about 80% of tasks through text or voice commands alone.

241
00:21:17,199 --> 00:21:19,719
And this moment is not here yet.

242
00:21:19,719 --> 00:21:26,079
It could arrive in two to three years if things move fast or five to 10 years if things move slowly.

243
00:21:26,079 --> 00:21:28,399
Rebecca, what do you think of his comment?

244
00:21:28,399 --> 00:21:30,479
Is he being optimistic or realistic?

245
00:21:31,480 --> 00:21:38,279
I would say probably optimistic about two to three years, maybe realistic about 10 years.

246
00:21:38,279 --> 00:21:43,839
From what I hear at that price tag, it's a big question mark.

247
00:21:43,839 --> 00:21:51,039
That 16,000 price tag that Steve mentioned, who's going to pay $16,000 for a robot?

248
00:21:51,039 --> 00:21:53,480
They don't really know how well it's going to work.

249
00:21:53,480 --> 00:21:59,920
From what I've seen, some folks I know have tried it out and they're not too happy with

250
00:21:59,920 --> 00:22:02,720
the way it can handle household chores.

251
00:22:02,720 --> 00:22:09,320
So I think the real promise is the industrial side is in the factories, doing the dirty and the dangerous

252
00:22:09,320 --> 00:22:10,820
and the dull jobs.

253
00:22:10,320 --> 00:22:15,360
That's where the promise is and the profits are and the opportunity is.

254
00:22:15,360 --> 00:22:19,519
And China leads and China is the world's leading factory.

255
00:22:19,519 --> 00:22:21,160
It's the world's leading producer.

256
00:22:21,160 --> 00:22:22,660
So it makes sense.

257
00:22:22,600 --> 00:22:29,400
These humanoid robots could do these jobs in factories that humans don't want to do.

258
00:22:29,400 --> 00:22:33,000
And that the robot is better equipped to do.

259
00:22:33,000 --> 00:22:34,680
It's not going to be harmed.

260
00:22:34,680 --> 00:22:37,640
It's hard to harm a robot.

261
00:22:37,640 --> 00:22:41,920
Although I know that their batteries only last so long and they do have to rest as well.

262
00:22:41,920 --> 00:22:47,680
So with humans, we need to rest, but maybe not quite as often as two hours every time

263
00:22:47,680 --> 00:22:50,480
a robot needs to take a nap.

264
00:22:50,480 --> 00:22:56,200
Yeah, well, certainly we are seeing robots sorting through parcels, right?

265
00:22:56,200 --> 00:23:00,080
And they can work much, much longer than a human being can do.

266
00:23:00,080 --> 00:23:01,880
It's very monotonous.

267
00:23:01,880 --> 00:23:06,759
I don't see anybody, if they had a choice, would ever want to do that kind of job.

268
00:23:06,759 --> 00:23:13,160
But again, given this moment, it seems that there is still some interest, if not huge

269
00:23:13,160 --> 00:23:17,720
interest in purchasing these humanoid robots from China.

270
00:23:17,720 --> 00:23:23,080
And because of the reasons that we all mentioned, Steve, that Unitry and some other Chinese

271
00:23:23,079 --> 00:23:30,519
companies have been really at the leading pack of sales in terms of humanoid robots at this moment.

272
00:23:30,519 --> 00:23:38,159
The number is somewhere between 80 to 90 percent, even higher of global sales that are coming from China.

273
00:23:38,159 --> 00:23:45,039
Last year, I know Unitry sold about one-third of the number of humanoid robots that were

274
00:23:45,039 --> 00:23:47,879
shipped out of China, 5,500 units.

275
00:23:47,880 --> 00:23:55,000
What do you think of such a large market share being occupied by China?

276
00:23:55,000 --> 00:23:56,500
The upside?

277
00:23:56,000 --> 00:23:57,500
The downside?

278
00:23:57,000 --> 00:24:00,760
Sorry, the 5,000 mark?

279
00:24:00,760 --> 00:24:02,260
That's not a very big number.

280
00:24:02,240 --> 00:24:03,740
Steve, okay.

281
00:24:03,240 --> 00:24:04,740
This was for Steve.

282
00:24:04,240 --> 00:24:05,740
Sorry, I didn't make it clear.

283
00:24:05,240 --> 00:24:07,440
Yeah, but Rebecca, you can add something later.

284
00:24:07,440 --> 00:24:08,940
Yeah.

285
00:24:08,440 --> 00:24:09,940
Okay.

286
00:24:09,440 --> 00:24:12,160
Yes, 5,000 is actually a small number.

287
00:24:12,160 --> 00:24:14,640
So the market is still very small.

288
00:24:14,640 --> 00:24:20,240
And the point I was making is for the future, why people are betting on it now.

289
00:24:20,240 --> 00:24:29,759
The fact is, the upside for China is that China has a chance to become the world's dominant

290
00:24:29,759 --> 00:24:32,160
player in robotics.

291
00:24:32,160 --> 00:24:36,320
And robots are the embodiment of AI in the physical world.

292
00:24:36,320 --> 00:24:39,520
They are going to drive massive change.

293
00:24:39,519 --> 00:24:46,079
China is already the world's leading manufacturer and has the most sophisticated and robust

294
00:24:46,079 --> 00:24:48,000
supply chains in the world.

295
00:24:48,000 --> 00:24:51,799
All of that put together, make it very hard for other countries to compete.

296
00:24:51,799 --> 00:24:53,639
The US is scared.

297
00:24:53,639 --> 00:25:03,079
The US just banned robots because it is afraid that China will dominate because China is

298
00:25:03,079 --> 00:25:06,079
delivering these robots at such a competitive price.

299
00:25:06,079 --> 00:25:08,279
And there's a fit of competition.

300
00:25:08,279 --> 00:25:09,779
Yes, Steve.

301
00:25:09,279 --> 00:25:11,079
Rivals of US companies.

302
00:25:11,079 --> 00:25:12,579
Sorry for interrupting here.

303
00:25:12,279 --> 00:25:15,119
I have about four minutes left and I want to ask this question.

304
00:25:15,119 --> 00:25:19,279
I think it's hanging on the lips of many people watching at this moment where you're saying

305
00:25:19,279 --> 00:25:23,519
China will be the leader in terms of humanoid robot.

306
00:25:23,519 --> 00:25:28,319
For a lot of people watching this, they think of one word fear.

307
00:25:28,319 --> 00:25:31,960
What would you like to say to them?

308
00:25:31,960 --> 00:25:39,200
I would say that for most people, it doesn't matter where the robot comes from.

309
00:25:39,200 --> 00:25:40,700
The robots are coming.

310
00:25:40,559 --> 00:25:42,059
They are coming.

311
00:25:41,880 --> 00:25:46,000
So they are going to come from China or they are going to come from the US.

312
00:25:46,000 --> 00:25:49,680
No matter what, those two countries are going to be competing.

313
00:25:49,680 --> 00:25:52,960
We are going to have to learn to live with robots.

314
00:25:52,960 --> 00:25:59,000
But you don't have to fear too much right away because it is, like Rebecca pointed out,

315
00:25:59,000 --> 00:26:01,400
going to take a considerable amount of time.

316
00:26:01,400 --> 00:26:05,800
I speak with so many CEOs, top CEOs of robotics companies.

317
00:26:05,800 --> 00:26:11,000
They all tell me the biggest issue right now, the number one issue besides the battery life,

318
00:26:11,000 --> 00:26:13,320
they can solve that is maintenance.

319
00:26:13,320 --> 00:26:15,560
These robots break down all the time.

320
00:26:15,560 --> 00:26:18,560
They are hard to maintain, hard to repair.

321
00:26:18,560 --> 00:26:20,360
They have many moving parts.

322
00:26:20,360 --> 00:26:21,860
This is the huge challenge.

323
00:26:21,680 --> 00:26:23,180
All right.

324
00:26:22,680 --> 00:26:27,880
Well, Rebecca, when I talk about fear, I'm not just talking about technology, right?

325
00:26:27,880 --> 00:26:34,000
Sometimes the more advanced the technology, the greater fear it is because people are fearing things such as ideology.

326
00:26:34,000 --> 00:26:35,800
You have covered China so much.

327
00:26:35,800 --> 00:26:38,000
You have talked to so many Chinese CEOs.

328
00:26:38,000 --> 00:26:42,440
How would you react to this kind of skepticism or suspicion?

329
00:26:42,440 --> 00:26:49,080
Well, I think people around the world are open to wherever they can get the best technology.

330
00:26:49,080 --> 00:26:54,280
It's not necessarily a negative thing toward China just because China could make probably

331
00:26:54,280 --> 00:26:56,520
the best humanoid robots right now.

332
00:26:56,920 --> 00:27:01,879
It's not going to be a negative, but I think long term, it's whoever can.

333
00:27:01,879 --> 00:27:03,720
We're in a global market space.

334
00:27:03,720 --> 00:27:09,160
Whoever can do the best product at the best price, they're going to win.

335
00:27:09,160 --> 00:27:11,000
Now, right now, we're in a competition.

336
00:27:11,000 --> 00:27:16,200
China and US are in this incredible competition with one another over technology.

337
00:27:16,200 --> 00:27:21,720
So some of that dynamic, which I just mentioned about the global marketplace is a little out

338
00:27:21,720 --> 00:27:26,600
of the culture with China and US being such ferocious competitors.

339
00:27:26,600 --> 00:27:31,400
But I think because we have regulations coming into play too.

340
00:27:31,400 --> 00:27:34,920
So we have blocks on exports and imports, trade tariffs.

341
00:27:34,920 --> 00:27:40,360
We've got a wargo, superpower war going on between these China and US and tech.

342
00:27:40,360 --> 00:27:47,000
So that plays into this whole idea of global marketplace and whoever has the best product

343
00:27:47,000 --> 00:27:49,559
at the best price wins.

344
00:27:49,559 --> 00:27:53,639
Because right now, it is really just China and the US in technology.

345
00:27:54,200 --> 00:28:02,200
Professor Liu, for the people who are watching this in Brazil, in Panama, in India, somewhere else,

346
00:28:03,000 --> 00:28:04,759
what is it going to mean for them?

347
00:28:04,759 --> 00:28:10,679
Because they're not going to have the kind of ecosystem to produce these high-tech products.

348
00:28:11,960 --> 00:28:13,799
How are they going to be affected?

349
00:28:13,799 --> 00:28:16,679
Keep it short, one minute maximum.

350
00:28:16,680 --> 00:28:23,400
Well, I think people watch with very keen interest of what is really there to be

351
00:28:23,400 --> 00:28:25,320
installed and what can be delivered.

352
00:28:25,960 --> 00:28:29,400
So this is really very much a consensus.

353
00:28:29,400 --> 00:28:36,759
But there are also some fears with regard to competition with who is really leading the world

354
00:28:36,759 --> 00:28:38,759
in the robot era.

355
00:28:38,759 --> 00:28:45,560
And there's also fear of past dependence and fear also from the watchdogs,

356
00:28:45,559 --> 00:28:52,919
where they may really feel that they are losing control over the past type of regulations,

357
00:28:52,919 --> 00:29:00,119
like when the driverless cars come to the road and how to regulate the traffic, etc.

358
00:29:00,119 --> 00:29:06,839
And there's also fear, more importantly, with regard to the new users, because they do not know

359
00:29:07,879 --> 00:29:14,440
what can really happen and if a certain robot really get out of control, what can be done.

360
00:29:14,440 --> 00:29:22,279
So I think it's really a mixture of excitement, the key interest, and also certain fears.

361
00:29:22,279 --> 00:29:24,920
But the future will be there.

362
00:29:24,920 --> 00:29:29,080
So that's something that we have a common understanding.

363
00:29:29,080 --> 00:29:30,759
That's what future is, right?

364
00:29:30,759 --> 00:29:32,440
Because we cannot know.

365
00:29:32,440 --> 00:29:34,840
All right, we have to leave at their time has ran out.

366
00:29:34,840 --> 00:29:39,480
Many thanks to my guests, Professor Liu Baocheng, Rebecca Fennin, and Steve Hoffman

367
00:29:39,480 --> 00:29:41,720
for sharing with us your valuable insight.

368
00:29:41,720 --> 00:29:43,220
Thank you so much.

369
00:29:42,920 --> 00:29:46,840
And many thanks to our viewers for having followed us on this edition of The Point

370
00:29:46,840 --> 00:29:49,720
with me, Liu Xin, coming to you live from Beijing as usual.

371
00:29:49,720 --> 00:29:53,000
You can follow me on Facebook, X and YouTube using the hand-to-Lixin in Beijing.

372
00:29:53,000 --> 00:29:55,320
On behalf of the whole team, thank you for watching.

373
00:29:55,320 --> 00:29:59,880
You've got the point.
